4","firstPublicationDate":"2011-06-14","firstIndexDate":"2011-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"20227409","source":"MED","pmid":"20227409","pmcid":"PMC2878924","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2878924"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.febslet.2010.03.017","title":"Circadian clock control of the cellular response to DNA damage.","authorString":"Sancar A, Lindsey-Boltz LA, Kang TH, Reardon JT, Lee JH, Ozturk N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sancar A","lastName":"Sancar","firstName":"Aziz","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics, University of North Carolina School of Medicine, Chapel Hill, NC 27599-7260, USA. Aziz_Sancar@med.unc.edu"}]}},{"fullName":"Lindsey-Boltz LA","lastName":"Lindsey-Boltz","firstName":"Laura A","initials":"LA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6493-8194"}},{"fullName":"Kang TH","lastName":"Kang","firstName":"Tae-Hong","initials":"TH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6013-900X"}},{"fullName":"Reardon JT","lastName":"Reardon","firstName":"Joyce T","initials":"JT"},{"fullName":"Lee JH","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Jin Hyup","initials":"JH"},{"fullName":"Ozturk N","lastName":"Ozturk","firstName":"Nuri","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5206-4127"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5206-4127"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6493-8194"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6013-900X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"584","journalIssueId":1734505,"dateOfPublication":"2010 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2010,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"FEBS Lett","title":"FEBS letters","essn":"1873-3468","issn":"0014-5793","isoabbreviation":"FEBS Lett","nlmid":"0155157"},"printPublicationDate":"2010-06-01"},"pubYear":"2010","pageInfo":"2618-2625","abstractText":"Mammalian cells possess a cell-autonomous molecular clock which controls the timing of many biochemical reactions and hence the cellular response to environmental stimuli including genotoxic stress. The clock consists of an autoregulatory transcription-translation feedback loop made up of four genes/proteins, BMal1, Clock, Cryptochrome, and Period. The circadian clock has an intrinsic period of about 24 h, and it dictates the rates of many biochemical reactions as a function of the time of the day. Recently, it has become apparent that the circadian clock plays an important role in determining the strengths of cellular responses to DNA damage including repair, checkpoints, and apoptosis. These new insights are expected to guide development of novel mechanism-based chemotherapeutic regimens.","affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics, University of North Carolina School of Medicine, Chapel Hill, NC 27599-7260, USA. Aziz_Sancar@med.unc.edu","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIEHS NIH HHS","acronym":"ES","grantId":"P30 ES010126","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM032833","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM031082","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"DNA Damage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA Repair Enzymes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Cycle","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Apoptosis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA Repair","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mutation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Models, Biological"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Chronotherapy"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"DNA Repair Enzymes","registryNumber":"EC 6.5.1.-"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://cdr.lib.unc.edu/downloads/mp48sm074"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.febslet.2010.03.017"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC2878924"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC2878924?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.febslet.2010.03.017"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":242,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS187671","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2010-06-17","dateOfCreation":"2010-03-16","dateOfRevision":"2025-05-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2010-03-15","firstPublicationDate":"2010-03-15","firstIndexDate":"2010-06-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"22918252","source":"MED","pmid":"22918252","pmcid":"PMC3466558","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3466558"]},"doi":"10.4161/cc.21771","title":"Effect of circadian clock mutations on DNA damage response in mammalian cells.","authorString":"Gaddameedhi S, Reardon JT, Ye R, Ozturk N, Sancar A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gaddameedhi S","lastName":"Gaddameedhi","firstName":"Shobhan","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4978-6951"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics, University of North Carolina School of Medicine, Chapel Hill, NC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Reardon JT","lastName":"Reardon","firstName":"Joyce T","initials":"JT"},{"fullName":"Ye R","lastName":"Ye","firstName":"Rui","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Ozturk N","lastName":"Ozturk","firstName":"Nuri","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5206-4127"}},{"fullName":"Sancar A","lastName":"Sancar","firstName":"Aziz","initials":"A"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5206-4127"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4978-6951"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"18","volume":"11","journalIssueId":1958008,"dateOfPublication":"2012 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2012,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cell Cycle","title":"Cell cycle (Georgetown, Tex.)","essn":"1551-4005","issn":"1538-4101","isoabbreviation":"Cell Cycle","nlmid":"101137841"},"printPublicationDate":"2012-09-01"},"pubYear":"2012","pageInfo":"3481-3491","abstractText":"The circadian clock is a global regulatory mechanism that confers daily rhythmicity on many biochemical and physiological functions, including DNA excision repair in mammalian organisms. Here, we investigated the effect of the circadian clock on the major DNA damage response pathways by using mouse cell lines mutated in genes encoding proteins in the positive (Bmal1, CLOCK) or negative (Cry 1/2, Per 1/2) arms of the transcription-translation feedback loop that generates the circadian clock. We find that cells mutated in these genes are indistinguishable from wild-type in their response to UV, ionizing radiation and mitomycin C. We conclude that either the majority of DNA damage response reactions are not controlled by the circadian clock or that, even if such a control exists at the organism level, it is supplanted by homeostatic control mechanisms at the cellular level in tissue culture. We suggest that caution must be exercised in extrapolating from experiments in tissue culture to whole animals with respect to the effect of the circadian clock on cellular response to DNA damaging agents.","affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics, University of North Carolina School of Medicine, Chapel Hill, NC, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIEHS NIH HHS","acronym":"ES","grantId":"P30 ES010126","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM031082","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM032833","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"GM31082","orde